{"title":"Vector analysis","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p><i>Vector analysis</i> or <i>vector calculus</i> is the mathematical field dedicated to studying the methods of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Calculus\" title=\"Calculus\">calculus</a> such as differentiation and integration applied to <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vector_field&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Vector field (page does not exist)\">vector fields</a>. In modern mathematics, vector analysis is often taken to be sub-field of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Differential_geometry&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Differential geometry (page does not exist)\">differential geometry</a>. In terms of university course listings, it is common to use the word \"vector calculus\" synonymously with <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Multivariable_calculus&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Multivariable calculus (page does not exist)\">multivariable calculus</a>.\n</p><p>Application-wise, vector analysis plays significant roles in the sciences and engineering. In physics, vector analysis is used heavily in the study of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Electromagnetism\" title=\"Electromagnetism\">electromagnetism</a> among various other fields of physics. In engineering, vector analysis often shows up in the form of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cauchy_stress_tensor&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Cauchy stress tensor (page does not exist)\">Cauchy stress tensor</a>. Another significant application of vector analysis is to fluid dynamics. In particular, one can describe the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler_equations&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Euler equations (page does not exist)\">Euler equations</a> via the methods of vector analysis.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Classical_Vector_Analysis\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Classical Vector Analysis</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Scalar_fields\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Scalar fields</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Vector_fields\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Vector fields</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Differential_operators\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Differential operators</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-3 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Divergence\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Divergence</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-3 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Curl\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.3.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Curl</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-3 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Gradient\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.3.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Gradient</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-3 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Laplacian\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.3.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Laplacian</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#The_classical_cases_of_Stokes&#39;_Theorem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">The classical cases of Stokes' Theorem</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Classical_Vector_Analysis\">Classical Vector Analysis</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vector_analysis&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Classical Vector Analysis\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Classical vector analysis largely is based developing the methods of calculus for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba438cedaa8f58c80eb239e792bd4cdeba1910ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^3$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />. At the time when vector analysis was relatively new, it was common to perceive applied mathematics from only a 3-dimensional perspective as it is commonly propagated that the physical space that is observable is that of 3-dimensional Euclidean space. This is not completely true and necessitates generalizations of traditional vector analysis.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Scalar_fields\">Scalar fields</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vector_analysis&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Scalar fields\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>A <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Scalar_field&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Scalar field (page does not exist)\">scalar field</a> is traditionally a map <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/9/219867d095ddd28d32609bcd0577fa1486196a57.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f:U\\to\\mathbb{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"83\" height=\"18\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" /> is a subset of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/9/359c93f77ee9ad4fdfaae8b7c46a2d7ed91a3b29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^n$\" width=\"21\" height=\"12\" />. In other words, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is a map that assigns a real-valued scalar to every point in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" />. Generally, this scalar will represent some type of quantity such as <i>potential</i> in physics. Mainly, scalar fields are generalizations of functions with more variables: A function f(x) in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/a/00a62d72646c467ae02878bc6dd7138e087a13c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^2$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> can be implicitly defined as a scalar field F(x, y) = x + f(x). The <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/a/00a62d72646c467ae02878bc6dd7138e087a13c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^2$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> scalar field can be represented using a multivariable function that represents a graph in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba438cedaa8f58c80eb239e792bd4cdeba1910ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^3$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> where z = f(x, y) = the scalar produced by the corresponding scalar field in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/a/00a62d72646c467ae02878bc6dd7138e087a13c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^2$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Vector_fields\">Vector fields</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vector_analysis&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Vector fields\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>A <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vector_field&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Vector field (page does not exist)\">vector field</a> in the traditional sense is a map <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/f/91f9791ce2e3b0089580c1ecff67aff86e8c0879.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{F}:u\\to\\mathbb{R}^3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"89\" height=\"17\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" /> is a subset of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba438cedaa8f58c80eb239e792bd4cdeba1910ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^3$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />. That is, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> associates a vector in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba438cedaa8f58c80eb239e792bd4cdeba1910ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^3$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> to every point in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" />. Typically, this construction is used to represent some kind of direction along with a quantity being associated with to a specific point in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba438cedaa8f58c80eb239e792bd4cdeba1910ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^3$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />. Vector fields are dimensionless relations respect to distance. This may be electric fields, magnetic fields, or even fields that model fluid flow (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/2/1c2c21bd5fa15d8f04a93ad369d15f4a43ae380c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{E}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/4/684089822af19d4ccbfae10139f057dbdad68d9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{B}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b5570d4a8d6f360676737b5eb2f492204b83f617.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{u}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />). For a given vector field <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/1/a01b1c51e370cb2f12af2152cd3c9d61e9fec928.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{F}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, it has a flow of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/c/38c95b75075a21ed6c74a473f9db7d84c5f5c86d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\int_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{t}\\,ds$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"81\" height=\"42\" /> and a flux <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/5/b85bbfb165b853d6f5f9734be4cdabbb453f4e43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Phi$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/7/ca7f6bfccc529671e72594170e6a5263c94429ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\iint_{E}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{n}\\,dS$\" style=\"vertical-align: -24px\" width=\"91\" height=\"45\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Differential_operators\">Differential operators</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vector_analysis&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Differential operators\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>In the world of vector analysis, various forms of differential operators exist. Most notable are that of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Divergence\" title=\"Divergence\">divergence</a>, <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Curl&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Curl (page does not exist)\">curl</a>, the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Gradient\" title=\"Gradient\">gradient</a>, and the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Laplacian\" title=\"Laplacian\">Laplacian</a>.\n</p>\n<h4><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Divergence\">Divergence</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vector_analysis&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Divergence\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h4>\n<p>Divergence of a vector field measures how the vectors are \"diverging out\". If the divergence is positive, the vector field is considered a \"source\", and if it is negative, it is considered a \"sink\". If it is divergence-free, the vector field is incompressible. It is also the density of outward <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Flux&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Flux (page does not exist)\">flux</a> at a given point in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba438cedaa8f58c80eb239e792bd4cdeba1910ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^3$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />. Under this definition, divergence is often thought of as 'flux density' which is the motivation for the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Divergence_theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Divergence theorem (page does not exist)\">Divergence theorem</a>.\n</p><p><b>Definition</b>: Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/8/d180254565de8d2f08e9be1fbbcb61898d9c9221.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{F}:U\\to\\mathbb{R}^3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"93\" height=\"17\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" /> is a subset of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba438cedaa8f58c80eb239e792bd4cdeba1910ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^3$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />. Then the <i>divergence</i> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/1/a01b1c51e370cb2f12af2152cd3c9d61e9fec928.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{F}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> evaluated at the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/a/7eaee7feea7d79181787a633a0686e5fdcfe8b7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p\\in U$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"46\" height=\"16\" /> is given by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/7/d77a6dd80149784131a83da3052efbdd34fd7097.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{div}(\\mathbf{F})(p) = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{F} = \\lim_{V(R)\\to 0} \\frac{1}{V(R)}\\iint_{\\partial R}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{n}\\,dS,\\]\" width=\"358\" height=\"46\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is a region whose volume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/0/fb000d328423eea10a9994ad79802e1d1c81e757.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V(R)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"41\" height=\"18\" /> shrinks to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> about the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/a/20aee8effb5e37e28e0b2bf579602311e7da45a0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\partial R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"26\" height=\"14\" /> is the boundary of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/f/0cf40500c62874887969d031e32be4bfc7a9c519.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is the outward unit normal of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/1/a01b1c51e370cb2f12af2152cd3c9d61e9fec928.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{F}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> relative to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/2/3e2c53dbc3c17c33d08b41dccb15128506af23dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$dS$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"23\" height=\"14\" /> is an area element of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h4><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Curl\">Curl</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vector_analysis&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Curl\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h4>\n<p>Curl of a vector field is the density of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circulation&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Circulation (page does not exist)\">circulation</a> at a given point in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba438cedaa8f58c80eb239e792bd4cdeba1910ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^3$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />. It can also be known as the \"rotor\" of the vector field, as it measures how the vectors are \"spinning\". Thus, one can think of curl intuitively as 'circulation density' which is motivation for <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Stokes%27_Theorem\" title=\"Stokes&#39; Theorem\">Stokes' Theorem</a>.\n</p><p><b>Definition</b>: Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/8/d180254565de8d2f08e9be1fbbcb61898d9c9221.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{F}:U\\to\\mathbb{R}^3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"93\" height=\"17\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" /> is a subset of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba438cedaa8f58c80eb239e792bd4cdeba1910ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^3$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />. Then the curl of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/1/a01b1c51e370cb2f12af2152cd3c9d61e9fec928.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{F}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> evaluated at the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/a/7eaee7feea7d79181787a633a0686e5fdcfe8b7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p\\in U$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"46\" height=\"16\" /> is given by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/d/61d38f8c9564acc1d31e25f45d8ad8afa73fdcb6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{curl}(\\mathbf{F})(p)\\cdot\\mathbf{n} = \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{F} \\cdot\\mathbf{n} =  \\lim_{A(R)\\to 0} \\frac{1}{A(R)}\\int_{\\partial R}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{t}\\,ds,\\]\" width=\"419\" height=\"41\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is a region whose area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/3/f536d890df884fa61b69983ebe1f845fb0a1e2e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A(R)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"40\" height=\"18\" /> shrinks to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> about the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the boundary (closed loop) of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/f/0cf40500c62874887969d031e32be4bfc7a9c519.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is the outward unit normal of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/1/a01b1c51e370cb2f12af2152cd3c9d61e9fec928.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{F}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> relative to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/2/e32f19fc37516d5df12ef5c0c7d509b5e7464c21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{t}$\" width=\"7\" height=\"12\" /> is the unit tangent of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/1/a01b1c51e370cb2f12af2152cd3c9d61e9fec928.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{F}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> relative to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/4/804a8d1c4e19e5a78fd601c01cc26e9f07e44f66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ds$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> is an arclength element of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>An alternative definition that one can give that does not rely upon the normal component of curl is as follows:\n</p><p><b>Definition</b>: Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/8/d180254565de8d2f08e9be1fbbcb61898d9c9221.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{F}:U\\to\\mathbb{R}^3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"93\" height=\"17\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" /> is a subset of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba438cedaa8f58c80eb239e792bd4cdeba1910ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^3$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />. Then the <i>curl</i> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/1/a01b1c51e370cb2f12af2152cd3c9d61e9fec928.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{F}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> evaluated at the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/a/7eaee7feea7d79181787a633a0686e5fdcfe8b7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p\\in U$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"46\" height=\"16\" /> is given by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/c/3acd5baf741033290ee75959d4dec7ff259b98ab.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{curl}(\\mathbf{F})(p) = \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{F} = \\lim_{V(R)\\to 0} \\frac{1}{V(R)}\\iint_{\\partial R}\\mathbf{F}\\times\\mathbf{n}\\,dS,\\]\" width=\"381\" height=\"46\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is a region whose volume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/0/fb000d328423eea10a9994ad79802e1d1c81e757.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V(R)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"41\" height=\"18\" /> shrinks to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> about the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/a/20aee8effb5e37e28e0b2bf579602311e7da45a0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\partial R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"26\" height=\"14\" /> is the boundary of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/f/0cf40500c62874887969d031e32be4bfc7a9c519.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is the outward unit normal of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/1/a01b1c51e370cb2f12af2152cd3c9d61e9fec928.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{F}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> relative to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/2/e32f19fc37516d5df12ef5c0c7d509b5e7464c21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{t}$\" width=\"7\" height=\"12\" /> is the unit tangent of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/1/a01b1c51e370cb2f12af2152cd3c9d61e9fec928.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{F}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> relative to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/2/3e2c53dbc3c17c33d08b41dccb15128506af23dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$dS$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"23\" height=\"14\" /> is an area element of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h4><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Gradient\">Gradient</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vector_analysis&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Gradient\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h4>\n<p>The gradient of a scalar field <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/2/4e2c8319d40dfa6debd85561b1c170491b3a9f70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f:\\mathbb{R}^3\\to\\mathbb{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"20\" /> is typically interpreted to be the operator that gives the vector whose direction is that in the direction of greatest increase of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> and magnitude being the rate at which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is increasing. Gradient can be considered as the \"derivative vector\" of a given multivariable function. For example, a gradient in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/a/55a5d38bf4db95b3ea934b735046da46eade6efa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f:\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"20\" /> serves as a potential for a function in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba438cedaa8f58c80eb239e792bd4cdeba1910ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^3$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />. Gradient vector fields are irrotational; that is, the curl of the gradient equals the zero (point) vector field. As such, we can define the gradient in a similar way to divergence and curls as below:\n</p><p><b>Definition</b>: Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/9/219867d095ddd28d32609bcd0577fa1486196a57.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f:U\\to\\mathbb{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"83\" height=\"18\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" /> is a subset of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba438cedaa8f58c80eb239e792bd4cdeba1910ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^3$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />. Then the <i>gradient</i> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> evaluated at the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/a/7eaee7feea7d79181787a633a0686e5fdcfe8b7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p\\in U$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"46\" height=\"16\" /> is given by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/b/89bc2b1a5975636c703e87fecd4baeb53ba871c9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{grad}(f)(p) = \\nabla f = \\lim_{V(R)\\to 0} \\frac{1}{V(R)}\\iint_{\\partial R}f\\mathbf{n}\\,dS,\\]\" width=\"335\" height=\"46\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is a region whose volume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/0/fb000d328423eea10a9994ad79802e1d1c81e757.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V(R)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"41\" height=\"18\" /> shrinks to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> about the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/a/20aee8effb5e37e28e0b2bf579602311e7da45a0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\partial R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"26\" height=\"14\" /> is the boundary of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/f/0cf40500c62874887969d031e32be4bfc7a9c519.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is the outward unit normal relative to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/2/3e2c53dbc3c17c33d08b41dccb15128506af23dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$dS$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"23\" height=\"14\" /> is an area element of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. The differential <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/e/abed9dcd801e073b9e9377128039825c1c4cc065.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$df$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"18\" /> of a multivariable function is defined as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/1/1318551d50d325d5ff4d37981bb37146943865f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\nabla f \\cdot d\\mathbf{r}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p>\n<h4><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Laplacian\">Laplacian</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vector_analysis&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Laplacian\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h4>\n<p>The Laplacian of a scalar field <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/2/4e2c8319d40dfa6debd85561b1c170491b3a9f70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f:\\mathbb{R}^3\\to\\mathbb{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"20\" /> is typically defined as the divergence of the gradient of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> as below:\n</p><p><b>Definition</b>: Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/2/4e2c8319d40dfa6debd85561b1c170491b3a9f70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f:\\mathbb{R}^3\\to\\mathbb{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"20\" /> be a scalar field. Then the <i>Laplacian</i> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is given by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/a/89ada2a8350ae6ddd3f5743bd1697c298a4f9a68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{div}(\\text{grad}(f)) = \\nabla \\cdot \\nabla f = f \\nabla \\cdot \\nabla = \\nabla^2f.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"315\" height=\"21\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"The_classical_cases_of_Stokes.27_Theorem\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"The_classical_cases_of_Stokes'_Theorem\">The classical cases of Stokes' Theorem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vector_analysis&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: The classical cases of Stokes&#39; Theorem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>In classical vector analysis, several integral theorems use Stoke's Theorem.\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Vector_analysis&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018113517\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.041 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.062 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 605/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 286/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 19503/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.353      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.353      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:23213:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018113517 and revision id 240669. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}